WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.501
Narrator: While filming this scene

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.536
from Disney's "The Mandalorian,"

00:00:03.536 --> 00:00:05.605
the actors could see their surroundings,

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:08.008
but the surroundings
weren't actually there.

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:10.877
All of this is just LED screens

00:00:10.877 --> 00:00:13.680
displaying backgrounds pre-made
in a video game engine.

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:16.649
Compare that with this fight scene

00:00:16.649 --> 00:00:18.351
from "Avengers: Endgame."

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:20.587
Where actors jumped
around in a sea of green,

00:00:20.587 --> 00:00:23.423
imagining how VFX artists
would make this planet look

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:25.358
once filming had ended.

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:28.061
"The Mandalorian" is one of
the first major productions

00:00:28.061 --> 00:00:30.630
to choose LED walls over green screens.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.931
And the benefits for the actors

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:34.100
are just the tip of the iceberg.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.903
LED walls make the lighting
better, filming smoother,

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:38.805
and in certain cases, cost a lot less

00:00:38.805 --> 00:00:40.306
than using green screens.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.809
But to understand why the
team behind "The Mandalorian"

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:44.377
chose these LED screens,

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:47.113
we have to understand just how they work.

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:49.315
"Mandalorian" showrunner Jon Favreau

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.584
revolutionized virtual
production while directing

00:00:51.584 --> 00:00:53.753
"The Lion King" and "The Jungle Book."

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.755
However, the process for these two remakes

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:57.557
still relied heavily on blue screen

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:59.459
and post-production work.

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.728
For "The Mandalorian,"
LED wall technologies

00:01:01.728 --> 00:01:03.430
seemed like the next logical step

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.865
given the show's production
budget and time frame.

00:01:06.933 --> 00:01:08.401
Now, you may be thinking,

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:11.337
'This isn't so new, I've seen
something like this before.'

00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:13.206
And you're right, kind of.

00:01:13.206 --> 00:01:15.308
The predecessor to what we
see on "The Mandalorian"

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.777
is a driving scene like
this one, from "Dr. No."

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.212
You've got the actor in the car 

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:20.947
and behind them, a screen with footage

00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.215
of the road they've traveled.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:24.017
But the technology was limited.

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:25.518
Say you want to move the camera angle

00:01:25.518 --> 00:01:26.419
during the scene.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:27.854
That projected footage can't

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:29.155
move with the camera.

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:30.857
But by using Unreal Engine,

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:32.992
tech borrowed from the video game field,

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:34.594
that problem is solved.

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:37.630
Artists can create a
photorealistic 3D background

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:38.731
that moves strictly with

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:39.966
the camera's field of view,

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:41.367
known as the frustum.

00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.970
So if the camera swings
around and changes angles,

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:47.173
the background shifts in
precisely the same way.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:48.942
This allows motion-tracked cameras

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:51.277
to execute traditional
cinematography techniques

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:53.313
within the virtual set,

00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:54.647
achieving cinematic movements

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:57.383
like the parallax effect, where
an object in the foreground

00:01:57.383 --> 00:01:58.818
moves at a different
speed than the background,

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:02.489
amplifies the illusion of filming
at an actual location.

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.391
Now that LED screens can
move with the camera's eye,

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:08.862
virtual sets can solve a bunch
of green screen problems.

00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:11.798
The biggest one probably being lighting.

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:15.201
Richard Bluff: We've all
seen movies, TV shows,

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.303
where the lighting on the day

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:19.639
didn't necessarily match
the post-production work

00:02:19.639 --> 00:02:22.509
that was added many, many months later.

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:23.676
Narrator: That's Richard Bluff,

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:26.012
Industrial Light and
Magic's VFX supervisor

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:27.113
for "The Mandalorian."

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.883
He says lighting is
one of the key benefits

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.684
of working with virtual sets.

00:02:31.684 --> 00:02:32.986
The light coming from the LEDs

00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:35.088
provide realistic colors and reflections

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:36.656
on the actors and props,

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:39.292
something you simply can't
achieve with green screen.

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:42.495
They are also able to completely
avoid the problem of spill.

00:02:42.495 --> 00:02:43.596
To better understand that,

00:02:43.596 --> 00:02:45.999
we talked to Kim Libreri at Epic Games,

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:47.500
creators of Unreal Engine,

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.335
which was used to build the virtual sets

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:50.770
for "The Mandalorian."

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:51.838
Kim Libreri: The problem
with the green screen

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:54.307
is that you have to have
a green screen behind you.

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:56.776
And what that does is
that instead of projecting

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:58.945
beautiful lighting for
what the environment

00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:00.446
would be behind you,

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:02.849
it basically puts a lot
of green light on you.

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:04.784
And we call that "spill."

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:08.521
If you wrap an actor with
a big 360 LED wall,

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:09.822
you can light in a way

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:12.725
that you would never be able
to do on a green screen.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:14.227
So you can really make it feel

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:16.763
like the characters are
embedded in their environments.

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:18.565
Narrator: This green screen spill

00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:20.500
would've been an even
bigger problem than usual

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.569
for "The Mandalorian,"
whose main character

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.204
gets a shiny new suit of armor

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:25.338
early on in the season.

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:28.141
So realistic lighting from the LEDs

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:29.576
makes the show look better.

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:32.445
But virtual sets help with
practical concerns too.

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:34.581
Take this scene in the
office of The Client,

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:36.049
played by Werner Herzog.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:37.183
In a scene like this,

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.719
surely they just built
a simple set, right?

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:40.587
Wrong.

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:43.623
All of this is that same LED screen.

00:03:43.623 --> 00:03:44.457
According to Richard,

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.893
only the desk, the floor,
and one or two columns

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:48.294
were really there.

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:50.763
Richard: Everything else
was displayed on the screen.

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:52.332
But this allowed us to limit

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:54.500
the amount of stage space that we used

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.402
in shooting season one of "Mandalorian,"

00:03:56.402 --> 00:04:01.241
which has, understandably, a
big impact on time and budget.

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:03.409
Narrator: To be fair,
the initial investment

00:04:03.409 --> 00:04:05.778
in a virtual set like this is huge.

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:07.680
However, the creators
claim that money saved

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.049
from traveling to locations,
building new sets,

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.184
and costly post-production,

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:12.819
can make the investment worth it.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.688
Along with saving time and money,

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:16.923
the team developed a system

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.691
that allowed artists to make changes

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:21.494
and control the world
on the day of shooting.

00:04:21.494 --> 00:04:23.229
Settings like exposure, color,

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:25.231
animation playback, and fill lighting

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:28.468
are available to the filmmakers
at a moment's notice.

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:29.702
If they want to move a mountain

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:31.070
from one side of the virtual set

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:34.007
to the other, they can,
right there on set.

00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:35.842
Not only is it helpful for the actor,

00:04:35.842 --> 00:04:38.211
who no longer has to imagine
where that mountain is,

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:41.347
but it's a game changer for
the director of photography.

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:42.615
Richard: Because in the distance,

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:46.252
if there's a mountain range
that is a story point,

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.655
then the DP's no longer guessing

00:04:48.655 --> 00:04:50.256
where those elements are,

00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:51.758
he can frame up to them.

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:54.560
Narrator: The virtual set
constructed for season one

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.530
was 75 feet in diameter, 21 feet high,

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.899
and also had a roof composed of LEDs.

00:04:59.899 --> 00:05:03.503
The cast and crew referred to
this space as "The Volume."

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:06.072
Richard: And we had doors
that can kinda close in

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:10.009
to almost give you full 360-
degree coverage of LED screens,

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:11.244
minus the floor.

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:13.513
It's driven by a small group of machines

00:05:13.513 --> 00:05:18.184
running Unreal and our
StageCraft technologies.

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.186
Narrator: At the end of the
day, the big question is,

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:23.122
do virtual sets really mean
the end for green screens?

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:24.324
Well, even "The Mandalorian,"

00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:25.958
with it's technological advances,

00:05:25.958 --> 00:05:27.560
used a version of a green screen,

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:29.629
but via the LED wall.

00:05:29.629 --> 00:05:31.531
Another capability of virtual sets

00:05:31.531 --> 00:05:33.599
is to choose pinpointed areas of the wall

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:35.268
and turn those green.

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:38.538
Richard: Now, we now
have the ability to limit

00:05:38.538 --> 00:05:39.706
the amount of green screen

00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:41.441
that's visible behind the character.

00:05:41.441 --> 00:05:44.544
So now it doesn't wrap
around the entire stage,

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:47.347
it's just a little piece, but also,

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:49.749
they gain the lighting
from that environment.

00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:51.517
Which gives us a more seamless

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:53.186
result in post-production.

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:56.022
Narrator: Additionally,
scenes involving explosives

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:58.257
are best kept to traditional methods.

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:00.560
This is to prevent LEDs
from getting damaged.

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:05.198
So will green screens survive
this wave of tech innovation?

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:07.967
Richard: Eventually, of course, we hope

00:06:07.967 --> 00:06:09.602
never to use green screen.

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:12.338
But, I still see that there will be

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:14.741
a future for it in the short-term,

00:06:14.741 --> 00:06:18.077
because there's likely always
a need to remove people,

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:21.047
to add additional action behind them.

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:22.482
But we are getting to the point

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:25.518
where the amount of green
screen that's being used

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:28.254
is massively reduced

00:06:28.254 --> 00:06:30.390
and the sky's the limit right now.

